\title{Notes on formulating discrete-induced physics}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{fdip}
\tag{discrete-physics}
\tag{lattice}
\tag{induced-gravity}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{Physics often models length, time and field values as continuous
(infinitely divisible) quantities. Measurements have finite resolution,
so they do not establish that this continuum is fundamental. The real
numbers are its usual mathematical form. The hypothesis is that geometry,
time and matter can be induced from finite carriers and exact algebra,
with continuum physics recovered at large scales. A classical field
assigns values to points of space; in this discrete setting, the points
are vertices or edges of a finite graph. Finitely many coordinates need
not mean finitely many possible values: later
[Gaussian probability laws](fdip-0005) are laws on a real
inner-product space. What a structure induces is kept separate from what must be
supplied. A [finite incidence presentation](fdip-0002) records
the finite vertex set, edge set and endpoint maps; the alternating
four-cycle of \ref{fdip-0003}
makes the additional weights explicit. Reordering vertices changes a
representation; changing weights changes the model.
Gaussian elimination in [Elimination and retained observables](fdip-0018)
uses a specified quadratic action and reference measure.}

\p{Induced geometry provides one motivation. Shamir's dynamical random
spatial lattice yields an effective metric conditional on the existence
of suitable localized low-energy states
\citet{Abstract}{shamir1994dynamical}. Shamir's conditional metric
motivates separating supplied microscopic data, state assumptions and
induced structure. \ref{fdip-003X} asks for an arithmetic scheme with a
Clifford sheaf and a finite-group action, for a map from a moduli stack
controlling the continuum limit and the background dependence, and for
quantum-field-theory parameters given by special L-values. Spacetime, physical parameters, the Standard
Model equations and gravitational equations stay outside those finite
constructions. [Discrete induction and its open interfaces](fdip-003W)
records the comparisons that remain open: the physical environment map,
and the continuum question, which is asked and not answered.
The groupoid and the realization do not supply a site, a stack, or a
descent. No moduli map is constructed.}

\p{These notes develop finite constructions and do not propose a physical
theory. The parts run from fields on finite carriers through Gaussian laws,
symmetry and Clifford algebras, to arithmetic families and a fixed-mode
comparison on a supplied circle. Geometric realization then asks which
information survives when weights are forgotten. Selected backgrounds,
gauge fields, Euclidean time and lattice fermions give the finite
constructions that the structure chapters and the closing
\ref{fdip-003W} compare. \ref{fdip-004E} maps the parts. The
alternating four-cycle returns as the example that ties a later
construction to specified fields, weights and observables.}

